[\nS=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points that must lie on the three lines.  \nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is not \\(0\\), not \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and not \\(-1\\).  \nHence a non\u2011sunny line can be only of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\ny=c,\\qquad x=c,\\qquad x+y=c,\n\\]\n\nwith \\(c\\) an integer.\n\nAssume that three distinct lines \\(L,\\,L_{1},\\,L_{2}\\) satisfy the covering condition and that exactly two of them are sunny.  \nThus \\(L\\) is non\u2011sunny and \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(S\\)\n\nLet a line have slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nIf it contains three distinct points \\((a,b),(a',b'),(a'',b'')\\in S\\) then the differences of the \\(x\\)-coordinates are \\(1\\) or \\(2\\).  \nFrom \\(\\frac{b'-b}{a'-a}=m=\\frac{b''-b'}{a''-a'}\\) we obtain that the numerator and denominator must be proportional, which forces \\(m\\) to be \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \nHence a sunny line meets \\(S\\) in at most two points. \u220e\n\nConsequently, after removing the points covered by the non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\), the remaining points must be split into two disjoint pairs, each pair lying on a sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Exhaustion of the possible non\u2011sunny lines\n\nWe treat the three families of non\u2011sunny lines separately.\n\n#### (a) Horizontal lines \\(y=c\\)\n\n* \\(c=1\\): \\(L\\) covers \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\).  \n  Any line through two of them is either vertical (\\(x=1\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\); both are non\u2011sunny.  \n  Hence the two sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n* \\(c=2\\): \\(L\\) covers \\((1,2),(2,2)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \n  The only sunny line that can contain two of these points is the line through \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)).  \n  The two points left, \\((1,1)\\) and \\((3,1)\\), are horizontal, giving a non\u2011sunny line \u2013 contradiction.\n\n* \\(c=3\\): \\(L\\) covers only \\((1,3)\\).  \n  Five points remain, but by the observation above each sunny line can cover at most two points, so two sunny lines can cover at most four points.  \n  Hence impossible.\n\nThus no horizontal non\u2011sunny line works.\n\n#### (b) Vertical lines \\(x=c\\)\n\n* \\(c=1\\): \\(L\\) covers \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  Any line through two of them is either horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\); both are non\u2011sunny.\n\n* \\(c=2\\): \\(L\\) covers \\((2,1),(2,2)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\).  \n  As in the previous subcase, any sunny line can contain at most two of these points, so we would need two sunny lines each covering exactly two points.  \n  The only sunny pair is \\((1,3)\\) with \\((3,1)\\) (slope \\(-1\\) \u2013 prohibited).  \n  All other pairs are vertical or horizontal.  \n  Hence impossible.\n\n* \\(c=3\\): \\(L\\) covers only \\((3,1)\\).  \n  The remaining five points cannot be covered by two sunny lines for the same reason as in the case \\(c=3\\) for horizontals.\n\nThus no vertical non\u2011sunny line works.\n\n#### (c) Lines of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\)\n\nPossible values of \\(c\\) are \\(2,3,4\\).\n\n* \\(c=2\\): \\(L\\) contains only \\((1,1)\\).  \n  Five points remain \u2013 impossible as before.\n\n* \\(c=3\\): \\(L\\) contains \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  The only sunny pair among them is \\((1,1)\\) with \\((2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \n  The other two points, \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\), have slope \\(-1\\), which is non\u2011sunny.  \n  Hence we cannot obtain two sunny lines.\n\n* \\(c=4\\): \\(L\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  Any line through two of these three points is either vertical or horizontal, both non\u2011sunny.\n\nThus no line of slope \\(-1\\) works.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nEvery possible choice for the unique non\u2011sunny line leads to a contradiction with the requirement that the two remaining lines be sunny and together cover all points of \\(S\\).  \nTherefore a configuration of three distinct lines covering the required points with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist for \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3,\\;k=2\\text{ is impossible.}}\n\\]"
