ve those slopes).  \nThe only remaining possibilities are \\(m=0\\) (horizontal) or \\(m=-1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)), both of which are non‑sunny.  \nThus a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(S\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBecause we are looking for three distinct lines with exactly two of them sunny, there must be **one** non‑sunny line, call it \\(L\\), and two sunny lines, call them \\(L_1,L_2\\).  \nBy Lemma 1 each of \\(L_1,L_2\\) can cover at most two points of \\(S\\); therefore after removing the points lying on \\(L\\) there can be at most four points left.  \nIf more than four points remain, the covering is impossible.\n\nWe now examine every possible choice for the non‑sunny line \\(L\\) according to (1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Horizontal lines \\(y=c\\)\n\nThe possible values of \\(c\\) are \\(1,2,3\\).\n\n* **\\(c=1\\).**  \n  \\(L\\) contains \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\(\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}\\) (three points).  \n  Any line through two of them is either vertical (\\(x=1\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\); both are non‑sunny.  \n  Hence we cannot obtain two sunny lines.\n\n* **\\(c=2\\).**  \n  \\(L\\) contains \\((1,2),(2,2)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\(\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\}\\) (four points).  \n  The only sunny pair among these four is \\((1,3)\\) with \\((2,1)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)).  \n  The other two points, \\((1,1)\\) and \\((3,1)\\), are horizontal, i.e. lie on a non‑sunny line.  \n  Thus we cannot split the four points into two sunny pairs.\n\n* **\\(c=3\\).**  \n  \\(L\\) contains only \\((1,3)\\).  \n  Five points remain, exceeding the capacity of two sunny lines (Lemma 1).  \n\nHence no horizontal line works.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Vertical lines \\(x=c\\)\n\nThe possible values of \\(c\\) are \\(1,2,3\\).\n\n* **\\(c=1\\).**  \n  \\(L\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\(\\{(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\) (three points).  \n  Any line through two of them is either horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\); both are non‑sunny.\n\n* **\\(c=2\\).**  \n  \\(L\\) contains \\((2,1),(2,2)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\}\\) (four points).  \n  As in the previous case, the only possible sunny pair would have to avoid slope \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\); the only such pair is \\((1,3)\\) with \\((3,1)\\), whose slope is \\(-1\\) (non‑sunny).  \n  Hence no sunny pair exists, let alone two.\n\n* **\\(c=3\\).**  \n  \\(L\\) contains only \\((3,1)\\).  \n  Five points remain, again impossible by Lemma 1.\n\nThus no vertical line works.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Lines of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\)\n\nSince the smallest sum of coordinates in \\(S\\) is \\(2\\) and the largest is \\(4\\), the possible values are \\(c=2,3,4\\).\n\n* **\\(c=2\\).**  \n  \\(L\\) contains only \\((1,1)\\).  \n  Five points remain → impossible.\n\n* **\\(c=3\\).**  \n  \\(L\\) contains \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\(\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\) (four points).  \n  The only sunny pair among them is \\((1,1)\\) with \\((2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \n  The other two points, \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\), have slope \\(-1\\), which is non‑sunny.  \n  Hence we cannot obtain two sunny lines.\n\n* **\\(c=4\\).**  \n  \\(L\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) (three points).  \n  Any line through two of these three points is either vertical or horizontal, both non‑sunny.\n\nThus no line of slope \\(-1\\) works.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince every admissible non‑sunny line leads to a contradiction, there is **no** set of three distinct lines covering all points of \\(S\\) with exactly two of them sunny.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3,\\;k=2\\text{ cannot be realized.}}\n\\]"
