oints \\((1,b)\\) (\\(1\\le b\\le m+1\\)).  \n     The total number of lines is \\((m)+1=m+1\\) and the number of sunny lines is still \\(k\\).\n\n   * **(ii) Obtaining \\(k=m+1\\).**  \n     Start from the family \\(\\mathcal L_{m,m}\\) (all its \\(m\\) lines are sunny).  \n     Replace the vertical line \\(x=1\\) that was used in step (i) by a sunny line that also passes\n     through the point \\((1,1)\\).  \n     Because \\(m\\ge3\\), the line\n     \\[\n     S_{m+1}:\\; y=(m-1)x-(m-2)\n     \\]\n     has slope \\(m-1\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\); it is sunny, passes through \\((1,1)\\) and also through\n     \\((2,m-1)\\in\\mathcal P_{m+1}\\).  \n     All points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge2\\) are already covered by the \\(m\\) sunny lines of\n     \\(\\mathcal L_{m,m}\\) (they were chosen in the induction hypothesis so that each such point\n     lies on at least one of them – this is possible because the induction hypothesis guarantees a\n     covering of the whole set \\(\\mathcal P_m\\)).  \n     Hence the new family consists of the \\(m\\) sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{m,m}\\) together with\n     the additional sunny line \\(S_{m+1}\\); it contains exactly \\(m+1\\) sunny lines and covers\n     \\(\\mathcal P_{m+1}\\).\n\n   The two constructions together show that for \\(n=m+1\\) every integer \\(k\\) with\n   \\(0\\le k\\le m+1\\) can be realised.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   By the base case \\(n=3\\) and the inductive step, the statement holds for every \\(n\\ge3\\):\n   for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering\n   \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   No other values of \\(k\\) are possible because we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, so the number of\n   sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) and cannot be negative.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\