n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  \\text{point}&\\text{covering line}\\\\\\hline\n  (1,1)&x=1\\\\\n  (1,2)&y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\\\\n  (1,3)&y=-2x+5\\\\\n  (2,1)&y=-2x+5\\\\\n  (2,2)&x=1\\\\\n  (3,1)&y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L_{3,3}=\\{\\,y=x,\\;y=-2x+5,\\;x+2y=5\\,\\}.\n  \\]\n  The three slopes are \\(1,-2,-\\tfrac12\\); none equals \\(0,-1,\\infty\\), so all three lines are sunny.\n  Their incidences are:\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  \\text{point}&\\text{covering line}\\\\\\hline\n  (1,1)&y=x\\\\\n  (1,2)&x+2y=5\\\\\n  (1,3)&y=-2x+5\\\\\n  (2,1)&y=-2x+5\\\\\n  (2,2)&y=x\\\\\n  (3,1)&x+2y=5\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\nThus for \\(n=3\\) every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le3\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Inductive construction\n\nAssume that for some integer \\(m\\ge3\\) the following statement holds:\n\n> (**Induction hypothesis**)  \n> For each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le m\\) there exists a family\n> \\(\\mathcal L_{m,k}\\) of \\(m\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) and containing exactly\n> \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nWe prove that the same statement is true for \\(n=m+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Obtaining the values \\(k\\le m\\)\n\nDefine the *horizontal shift* operator\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Shift}(L)=\\{(x+1,y)\\mid (x,y)\\in L\\}.\n\\]\nIf \\(L\\) is a line, \\(\\operatorname{Shift}(L)\\) is a parallel line with the same slope.\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L'_{m,k}=\\{\\operatorname{Shift}(L)\\mid L\\in\\mathcal L_{m,k}\\}\\).\nBecause the map \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a+1,b)\\) sends \\(\\mathcal P_m\\) bijectively onto the subset\n\\(\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{m+1}\\mid a\\ge2\\}\\), the family \\(\\mathcal L'_{m,k}\\) covers all points of\n\\(\\mathcal P_{m+1}\\) whose first coordinate is at least \\(2\\).  \n\nNow add the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (which is non‑sunny).  The resulting family\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{m+1,k}= \\mathcal L'_{m,k}\\cup\\{\\,x=1\\,\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\((m)+1=m+1\\) lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) sunny lines of\n\\(\\mathcal L_{m,k}\\) remain sunny; the added line is non‑sunny.  Hence \\(\\mathcal L_{m+1,k}\\) satisfies the\nrequirements for the pair \\((n,k)=(m+1,k)\\) with any \\(k\\le m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Obtaining the value \\(k=m+1\\)\n\nWe start from the family \\(\\mathcal L_{m,m}\\) supplied by the induction hypothesis; it consists of\n\\(m\\) sunny lines and covers \\(\\mathcal P_m\\).\n\nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\nS_{m+1}:\\; y=(m-1)x-(m-2).\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(m-1\\); for \\(m\\ge3\\) we have \\(m-1\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\), so \\(S_{m+1}\\) is sunny.\nMoreover \\(S_{m+1}\\) passes through the two points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,m-1)\\).\n\nApply the shift described in §2.1 to the family \\(\\mathcal L_{m,m}\\) and obtain\n\\(\\mathcal L'_{m,m}\\), which covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_{m+1}\\) with first coordinate at least \\(2\\).\nIn particular the point \\((2,m-1)\\) (which belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_{m+1}\\) because\n\\(2+(m-1)=m+1\\)) is already covered by some line of \\(\\mathcal L'_{m,m}\\).\n\nNow replace the vertical line \\(x=1\\) that was used in §2.1 by the sunny line \\(S_{m+1}\\).\nThe new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{m+1,m+1}= \\mathcal L'_{m,m}\\cup\\{\\,S_{m+1}\\,\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(m\\) sunny lines from \\(\\mathcal L'_{m,m}\\) and the additional sunny line \\(S_{m+1}\\);\nhence it has exactly \\(m+1\\) sunny lines.\nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_{m+1}\\) are covered:\n\n* points with \\(a\\ge2\\) are covered by \\(\\mathcal L'_{m,m}\\);\n* the point \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(S_{m+1}\\);\n* any other point \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge2\\) satisfies \\(1+b\\le m+1\\), i.e. \\(b\\le m\\).\n  Such a point belongs to the set \\(\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{m+1}\\mid a\\ge2\\}\\) after the shift\n  \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-1,b)\\); consequently it is covered by a line of \\(\\mathcal L'_{m,m}\\).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_{m+1,m+1}\\) meets all requirements for the pair \\((n,k)=(m+1,m+1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the induction\n\nThe base case \\(n=3\\) supplies families \\(\\mathcal L_{3,k}\\) for all \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\nThe constructions of §§2.1 and 2.2 show that, assuming the statement true for \\(n=m\\),\nit is also true for \\(n=m+1\\) for every admissible \\(k\\).\nTherefore, by mathematical induction, the statement holds for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe have exactly \\(n\\) lines in any admissible configuration; consequently the number of sunny\nlines cannot exceed \\(n\\) and cannot be negative.  Hence the only possible values are the\nintegers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\), all of which have been shown to be attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
