\\},\n\\qquad \nN_n=|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\nDenote by \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (i.e. each point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of the family);\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nWe shall prove that for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) such a family exists, and that no other values of \\(k\\) are possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\nThe following four families realise the four admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L_{3,0}=\\{\\,x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;x+y=4\\,\\}.\n  \\]\n  All three lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); hence none is sunny.  \n  Every point of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) has a fixed sum \\(a+b\\in\\{2,3,4\\}\\) and therefore lies on the\n  corresponding line.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L_{3,1}=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\;y=-2x+5\\,\\}.\n  \\]\n  The first two lines are vertical (non‑sunny); the third line has slope \\(-2\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\) and\n  is sunny.  The three lines cover \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) as shown in the table below.\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  \\text{point}&\\text{covering line}\\\\\\hline\n  (1,1)&x=1\\\\\n  (1,2)&x=1\\\\\n  (1,3)&x=1\\\\\n  (2,1)&x=2\\\\\n  (2,2)&x=2\\\\\n  (3,1)&y=-2x+5\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n* **\\(k=2\\).**  \n  \\[\n  \\mathcal L_{3,2}=\\{\\,x=1,\\;y=-2x+5,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\,\\}.\n  \\]\n  The line \\(x=1\\) is non‑sunny; the other two have slopes \\(-2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\), both sunny.\n  Their incidences with the six points are:\n\n  \\[\n  \\b