e \\(x+y=t+1\\); after the replacement it is covered by \\(S_t\\).  \n   The point \\((t+2,1)\\) was covered by the diagonal line \\(x+y=t+3\\); after the replacement it is still covered, now by \\(S_t\\).  \n   All other points are untouched, because every other line of the family remains unchanged.  \n   Consequently the new family still covers the whole set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *Obtaining any prescribed number of sunny lines.*  \n   Choose any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) and replace the diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\) by the sunny lines \\(S_1,S_2,\\dots ,S_k\\).  \n   The resulting family \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}_n\\) consists of  \n\n   * the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny);\n   * the \\(n-1-k\\) untouched diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\) (non‑sunny);\n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n\n   Hence \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}_n\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and still covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *The case \\(k=n-1\\).*  \n   Replace the vertical line \\(x=1\\) by the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   T:\\;y=(n-2)x-(n-3),\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   which passes through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,n-1)\\) (both in \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)).  \n   Together with the \\(n-2\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_{n-2}\\) constructed above we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   * For every \\(n\\ge3\\) the construction above yields, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   * For \\(n\\ge4\\) a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines is impossible (diagonal argument).  \n   * For \\(n=3\\) the diagonal argument does not apply; indeed the explicit family  \n\n     \\[\n     \\{\\,y=x,\\;y=-2x+5,\\;x+2y=5\\,\\}\n     \\]\n\n     shows that \\(k=3\\) is attainable.  \n\n   Therefore the set of admissible 