ing family \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}_n\\) consists of  \n\n   * the untouched diagonal lines \\(x+y=s\\) for \\(s=k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\) (non\u2011sunny);\n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n\n   Hence \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}_n\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and still covers the whole set \\(P_n\\).\n\n   *The case \\(k=n-1\\).*  \n   Keep the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   V:\\;x=1,\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   (non\u2011sunny) and replace the remaining \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) by the sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   S_1,S_2,\\dots ,S_{n-2}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n   L:\\;y=-(n-2)x+(n-1).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L\\) passes through the two points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\), both belonging to \\(P_n\\); its slope \\(-(n-2)\\) is sunny.  \n   Together with the vertical line \\(V\\) the family contains exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and covers every point of \\(P_n\\) (the points with abscissa \\(1\\) are covered by \\(V\\); all other points lie on one of the lines listed in (5)).\n\n   *The case \\(n=3\\).*  \n   The three lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=x,\\qquad y=-2x+5,\\qquad x+2y=5\n   \\]\n\n   are all sunny and contain the three points of \\(P_3\\); therefore \\(k=3\\) is also attainable when \\(n=3\\).\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n\n   Consider the three \u201cborder\u201d sets  \n\n   \\[\n   X=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad\n   Y=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\qquad\n   D=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Every point of \\(P_n\\) belongs to at least one of these three sets.  \n   A sunny line is **not** parallel to any of the three border lines, therefore it meets each of the three sets in **at most one** point.  \n\n   Suppose that all \\(n\\) lines were sunny.  \n   Because \\(|X|=|Y|=|D|=n\\), each of the three sets would have to be intersected exactly once by each line; otherwise some point of a border set would remain uncovered.  \n   Consequently the \\(n\\) lines would give a bijection  \n\n   \\[\n   \\phi_X:X\\longrightarrow Y,\\qquad\n   \\phi_Y:Y\\longrightarrow D,\\qquad\n   \\phi_D:D\\longrightarrow X,\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   where a line containing the points \\(x\\in X,\\;y\\in Y,\\;d\\in D\\) realises the three correspondences simultaneously.\n\n   Write the points of the three sets as  \n\n   \\[\n   X=\\{(1,i)\\}_{i=1}^{n},\\qquad\n   Y=\\{(i,1)\\}_{i=1}^{n},\\qquad\n   D=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\}_{i=1}^{n}.\n   \\]\n\n   If a line contains \\((1,i)\\) and \\((j,1)\\) then its slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-i}{j-1}\\).  \n   For the same line to also contain a point of \\(D\\) we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1-i}{j-1}= \\frac{n+1-j-i}{j-i}\\; .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   After clearing denominators equation (7) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   (1-i)(j-i)=(j-1)(n+1-j-i).\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   The left\u2013hand side is a multiple of \\((i-1)\\) whereas the right\u2013hand side is a multiple of \\((j-1)\\).  \n   Because \\(2\\le i,j\\le n\\) and \\(n\\ge4\\), the only solution of (8) in integers is \\(i=j\\).  \n   But \\(i=j\\) would make the line vertical, contradicting the assumption that the line is sunny.  \n\n   Hence no sunny line can contain simultaneously a point of \\(X\\), a point of \\(Y\\) and a point of \\(D\\).  \n   Consequently it is impossible to cover all three border sets with only sunny lines when \\(n\\ge4\\).  \n   Therefore at least one of the \\(n\\) lines must be non\u2011sunny, i.e. \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n\n   * For every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (constructions in \u00a75).  \n   * For \\(n\\ge4\\) a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines does not exist (argument in \u00a76).  \n   * For \\(n=3\\) the configuration \\(\\{y=x,\\;y=-2x+5,\\;x+2y=5\\}\\) shows that \\(k=3\\) is also possible.\n\n   Hence the