(c\\in\\{k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) and \\((1,b)\\in D_c\\).  \n\n   \u2013 \\((a,1)\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le n\\) is treated symmetrically (using \\(H\\), the lines \\(S_i\\) and the diagonals \\(D_c\\)).\n\n   *Interior points* \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\).  \n   Their sum \\(c=a+b\\) satisfies \\(4\\le c\\le n+1\\).\n\n   \u2013 If \\(c\\ge k+3\\) then \\(c\\) belongs to the set (3) and \\((a,b)\\in D_c\\).  \n\n   \u2013 If \\(4\\le c\\le k+2\\) write \\(c=i+1\\) with \\(3\\le i\\le k+1\\).  \n     Then \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are respectively of the form  \n\n     \\[\n     a=j+1,\\qquad b=i-j+1\\quad\\text{for some }1\\le j\\le i-2 .\n     \\]\n\n     The point \\((j+1,1)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_j\\) (by definition (2)), and the point \\((1,i-j+1)\\) also lies on \\(S_j\\).  \n     Because a line is uniquely determined by two of its points, the whole line \\(S_j\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n     Hence every interior point with sum \\(\\le k+2\\) belongs to one of the sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n\n   Consequently every element of \\(P_n\\) is covered by at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines, and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny.\n\n4. **Impossibility of having all \\(n\\) lines sunny when \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n\n   Consider the three families  \n\n   \\[\n   X=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad\n   Y=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\qquad\n   Z=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Each family contains \\(n\\) points and every point of \\(P_n\\) belongs to at least one of them.  \n   A sunny line is not parallel to any of the three families, therefore it meets each family in **at most one** point.\n\n   Suppose all \\(n\\) lines were sunny.  \n   To cover the \\(n\\) points of \\(X\\) we would need at least \\(n\\) distinct lines, and the same holds for \\(Y\\) and for \\(Z\\).  \n   Because we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, each line would have to contain **one point of each** of the three families.\n\n   Let a line contain \\((1,u)\\in X\\) and \\((v,1)\\in Y\\) with \\(2\\le u,v\\le n\\).  \n   Its slope is  \n\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{1-u}{\\,v-1\\,}= -\\frac{u-1}{v-1}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   If the same line also contains a point \\((p,q)\\in Z\\) (so \\(p+q=n+1\\)), then  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{q-1}{p-v}=m .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(q=n+1-p\\) and eliminating \\(m\\) from (6)\u2013(7) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   (u-1)(p-v)=(v-1)(n+1-p-u).\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   The left\u2011hand side is a multiple of \\(u-1\\) and the right\u2011hand side a multiple of \\(v-1\\).  \n   Since \\(2\\le u,v\\le n\\) and \\(n\\ge4\\), the only integer solution of (8) is \\(u=v\\).  \n   But \\(u=v\\) forces the line to be vertical, i.e. parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, contradicting the assumption that the line is sunny.  \n\n   Hence no sunny line can contain simultaneously a point of each of the three families, and it is impossible to cover all points of \\(P_n\\) with \\(n\\) sunny lines when \\(n\\ge4\\).  \n   Therefore for \\(n\\ge4\\) we must have \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n5. **The case \\(n=3\\).**  \n\n   Here  \n\n   \\[\n   P_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The three lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;y=2x-1,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;y=3x-5\n   \\]\n\n   are all sunny and each passes through exactly one of the three points, so \\(k=3\\) is attainable.  \n   The construction of \u00a72 with \\(k=0,1,2\\) also works, giving the remaining admissible values.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n\n   * For every \\(n\\ge4\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the explicit construction of \u00a72).  \n   * For \\(n\\ge4\\) a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines does not exist (impossibility proof of \u00a74).  \n   * For \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) (the three\u2011sunny example shows that \\(k=3\\) is possib