ose all \\(n\\) lines were sunny.  \n> To cover the \\(n\\) points of \\(X\\) we would need at least \\(n\\) distinct lines, and the same holds for \\(Y\\) and for \\(Z\\).  \n> Because we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, each line would have to contain **one point of each** of the three families.”\n\n**Critical Error.**  \nA single line can intersect two families at the same point (e.g. \\((n,1)\\) belongs to both \\(Y\\) and \\(Z\\)).  \nThus the lower‑bound argument that forces each line to contain a *distinct* point from each family is invalid.\n\n> “Let a line contain \\((1,u)\\in X\\) and \\((v,1)\\in Y\\) with \\(2\\le u,v\\le n\\).  \n> Its slope is  \n> \\[\n> m=\\frac{1-u}{\\,v-1\\,}= -\\frac{u-1}{v-1}.\n> \\tag{6}\n> \\]”\n\n*Correct computation.*\n\n> “If the same line also contains a point \\((p,q)\\in Z\\) (so \\(p+q=n+1\\)), then  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{q-1}{p-v}=m .\n> \\tag{7}\n> \\]”\n\n*Correct relation.*\n\n> “Substituting \\(q=n+1-p\\) and eliminating \\(m\\) from (6)–(7) yields  \n> \\[\n> (u-1)(p-v)=(v-1)(n+1-p-u).\n> \\tag{8}\n> \\]”\n\n**Critical Error.**  \nEliminating \\(m\\) correctly gives  \n\\[\n(u-1)(p-v) = (v-1)(p-n),\n\\]  \nnot the displayed equation (8).  The algebraic manipulation in the solution is wrong.\n\n> “The left‑hand side is a multiple of \\(u-1\\) and the right‑hand side a multiple of \\(v-1\\).  \n> Since \\(2\\le u,v\\le n\\) and \\(n\\ge4\\), the only integer solution of (8) is \\(u=v\\).”\n\n**Critical Error.**  \nEven the corrected equation admits solutions with \\(u\\neq v\\); for instance taking \\(p=v=n\\) (so the point from \\(Z\\) coincides with the point from \\(Y\\)) satisfies the equation for any \\(u\\).  Hence the conclusion “the only solution is \\(u=v\\)” is false.\n\n> “But \\(u=v\\) forces the line to be vertical, i.e. parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, contradicting the assumption that the line is sunny.  \n> Hence no sunny line can contain simultaneously a point of each of the three families, and it is impossible 