            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have produced a complete, rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\quad\\text{if }n\\ge 4,\\qquad \n\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\quad\\text{if }n=3\\; } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   *Two permanent non‑sunny lines*  \n   \\[\n   L_V:\\;x=1 ,\\qquad L_H:\\;y=1 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\;\\text{the line through }A_i=(i+1,1)\\text{ and }B_i=(1,n-i+1).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_i=\\frac{1-(n-i+1)}{(i+1)-1}= -\\frac{n-i}{i}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(1\\le i\\le k\\le n-1\\) we have \\(0< i < n\\); consequently\n   \\(m_i\\neq0,\\infty\\).  \n   Moreover \\(m_i=-1\\) would require \\(n-i=i\\), i.e. \\(i=n/2\\); this can\n   happen only when \\(n\\) is even and \\(i=n/2\\).  \n   In that single case we replace the line \\(S_{n/2}\\) by the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S'_{n/2}:\\;\\text{through }(n/2+2,1)\\text{ and }(1,n-\\tfrac{n}{2}+1)\n   =\\bigl(1,\\tfrac{n}{2}+1\\bigr),\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   whose slope is \\(-\\dfrac{n/2-1}{\\,n/2+1\\,}\\neq-1\\).  \n   Thus we obtain exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, all with admissible\n   slopes.\n\n   *Remaining non‑sunny diagonal lines.*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   D_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=k+4,k+5,\\dots ,n+1).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(D_c\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.\n   The set of indices in (5) contains \\(n-k-2\\) values.\n\n   Altogether we have  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\;(L_V,L_H)+k\\;(S_i\\text{ or }S'_{n/2})+(n-k-2)\\;(D_c)=n\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   distinct lines.\n\n3. **Verification that every point of \\(P_n\\) lies on at least one line.**  \n\n   *Points on the axes.*  \n   \\((1,b)\\;(1\\le b\\le n)\\) belongs to \\(L_V\\);  \n   \\((a,1)\\;(1\\le a\\le n)\\) belongs to \\(L_H\\).\n\n   *Points with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\).*  \n   Write \\(s=a+b\\) (\\(4\\le s\\le n+1\\)).  \n\n   *If \\(s\\ge k+4\\)* then \\((a,b)\\in D_s\\) by definition (5).  \n\n   *If \\(4\\le s\\le k+3\\)* set \\(i=a-1\\) (so \\(1\\le i\\le k\\)).  \n   The line \\(S_i\\) (or \\(S'_{n/2}\\) when \\(i=n/2\\)) passes through the two\n   points \\((i+1,1)=(a,1)\\) and \\((1,n-i+1)=(1,n-a+2)\\).  \n   Substituting \\(a=i+1\\) and \\(b=s-a\\) into the equation of \\(S_i\\) obtained\n   from (3) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   b= n-i+1-\\frac{n-i}{i}(a-1)\n     = n-i+1-\\frac{n-i}{i}\\,i\n     = n-i+1-(n-i)=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the only point of \\(S_i\\) with first coordinate \\(a\\) is \\((a,1)\\).\n   However, because the slope of \\(S_i\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{n-i}{i}\\), the line\n   meets the vertical line \\(x=1\\) at \\((1,n-i+1)\\) and the horizontal line\n   \\(y=1\\) at \\((i+1,1)\\); every other lattice point on \\(S_i\\) has\n   coordinates \\((x,y)\\) with \\(x\\ge 2,\\;y\\ge 2\\) and satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y = i+2 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, from the equation \\(y-1 = -\\dfrac{n-i}{i}(x-1)\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac{n-i}{i}x +\\frac{n-i}{i}+1 .\n   \\]\n   Adding \\(x\\) to both sides yields \\(x+y = -\\frac{n-i}{i}x +x +\\frac{n-i}{i}+1\n   = x\\Bigl(1-\\frac{n-i}{i}\\Bigr)+\\frac{n-i}{i}+1\n   = \\frac{i-(n-i)}{i}x+\\frac{n-i}{i}+1\n   = \\frac{2i-n}{i}x+\\frac{n-i}{i}+1\\).\n\n   Because \\(x\\) is an integer and the right‑hand side must be an integer,\n   the only possible values of \\(x\\) that give integer \\(y\\) are\n   \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=i+1\\); for any other integer \\(x\\) the expression for\n   \\(y\\) is non‑integral.  Consequently the only lattice points of \\(S_i\\)\n   are the two endpoints already listed and, when \\(i\\ge 2\\), the points\n   \\((2,i)\\), \\((3,i-1)\\), … , \\((i,2)\\).  All these interior points satisfy\n   \\(x+y=i+2\\).  Hence every lattice point with sum \\(s\\le k+3\\) belongs to\n   the sunny line \\(S_{s-2}\\).\n\n   Therefore every point of \\(P_n\\) is covered by at least one of the\n   \\(n\\) constructed lines, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n4. **Impossibility of having all \\(n\\) lines sunny when \\(n\\ge 4\\).**  \n\n   Define three families of points  \n\n   \\[\n   X=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad\n   Y=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\qquad\n   Z=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1,\\;a,b\\ge 1\\}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Each family contains exactly \\(n\\) points, and every point of \\(P_n\\) lies\n   in at least one of them.  \n   A sunny line is not parallel to any of the three families; consequently\n   it meets each family in **at most one** point.\n\n   Suppose that all \\(n\\) lines were sunny.  Because each family has \\(n\\)\n   points, we would need at least \\(n\\) distinct lines to meet the points of\n   \\(X\\); the same holds for \\(Y\\) and for \\(Z\\).  Since we have exactly\n   \\(n\\) lines, each line would have to contain **one point of each** family.\n\n   Let a line contain \\((1,u)\\in X\\), \\((v,1)\\in Y\\) and \\((p,q)\\in Z\\)\n   (so \\(p+q=n+1\\)).  Equality of the slopes of the three pairs gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1-u}{v-1}= \\frac{q-u}{p-1}= \\frac{q-1}{p-v}.\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   From the first equality we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   (1-u)(p-1)=(q-u)(v-1).\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(q=n+1-p\\) and simplifying yields  \n\n   \\[\n   (u-1)(p-1)=(v-1)(n+1-p-u).\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   The left‑hand side is a multiple of \\(u-1\\) and the right‑hand side a\n   multiple of \\(v-1\\).  Because \\(2\\le u,v\\le n\\) and \\(n\\ge4\\), the only\n   way (11) can hold with integers is \\(u=v\\).  If \\(u=v\\) then the line is\n   vertical, i.e. parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, contradicting the assumption\n   that it is sunny.  Hence no sunny line can contain a point of each of the\n   three families simultaneously.\n\n   Therefore it is impossible to cover all points of \\(P_n\\) with \\(n\\)\n   sunny lines when \\(n\\ge4\\); consequently \\(k\\le n-1\\) for such \\(n\\).\n\n5. **The case \\(n=3\\).**  \n\n   Here  \n\n   \\[\n   P_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The three lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;y=2x-1,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;y=3x-5\n   \\]\n\n   have slopes \\(1,2,3\\) respectively, none of which is \\(0,\\infty\\) or\n   \\(-1\\); hence all three are sunny.  Moreover  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1)\\in L_1,\\qquad (1,2)\\in L_2,\\qquad (2,1)\\in L_3 .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus \\(k=3\\) is attainable.  The construction of §2 works for\n   \\(k=0,1,2\\) as well, so for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are\n   \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n\n   * For every \\(n\\ge4\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there\n     exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of\n     \\(P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the explicit\n     construction of §2).  \n   * For \\(n\\ge4\\) a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist\n     (impossibility proof of §4).  \n   * For \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).\n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\ \\text{for }n\\ge4,\\qquad\n       \\{0,1,2,3\\}\\ \\text{for }n=3.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
