  Consequently, for each admissible \\(k\\) we can obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines by turning \\(k_{\\max}-k\\) of the sunny lines into additional non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   Hence **all** integers between \\(0\\) and the maximal value listed in step\u202f5 are attainable.\n\n7. **Impossibility of exceeding the maximal value.**  \n   Suppose a configuration with more sunny lines existed.  \n   Then fewer than the numbers of extra vertical lines given in (6) would be present.  \n   Consequently there would be a column \\(c\\) whose height \\(h_c\\) satisfies \\(h_c>n-3\\) while no vertical line is placed in that column.  \n   By the pigeon\u2011hole principle a sunny line can meet that column in at most one point, so at least \\(h_c\\) distinct sunny lines would be required, contradicting the fact that only \\(n-3\\) sunny lines are available.  \n   Therefore the bounds obtained in step\u202f5 are optimal.\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nP_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Three unavoidable non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nX&=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\\\\nY&=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\\\\nZ&=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1,\\ a,b\\ge1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(P_n\\) belongs to at least one of the three sets\n\\(X,Y,Z\\).  \nA sunny line is not parallel to any of the three sides of the triangle,\nhence it meets each of the families \\(X,Y,Z\\) in **at most one point**.\nConsequently at least one line parallel to each side is required.\nWe fix the three non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_V:x=1,\\qquad L_H:y=1,\\qquad L_D:x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThese three lines already cover all points of \\(X\\cup Y\\cup Z\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The interior points  \n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nI_n=P_n\\setminus (X\\cup Y\\cup Z)\n     =\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge2,\\ a+b\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|I_n|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe remaining \\(n-3\\) lines must cover the set \\(I_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A necessary number of extra vertical lines  \n\nFor a column with abscissa \\(c\\;(2\\le c\\le n-2)\\) the interior points\nlying in that column are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(c,b)\\mid 2\\le b\\le n-c\\},\n\\qquad\\text{hence }h_c:=|\\,\\{(c,b)\\in I_n\\}\\,|=n-c-1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIf a column does **not** contain a vertical line, every interior point of\nthat column must be supplied by a sunny line.  \nBecause a sunny line is not vertical, it can contain at most one point\nof that column.  Therefore we must have  \n\n\\[\nn-3\\;\\ge\\;h_c\\qquad\\text{for every column without a vertical line.}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe function \\(h_c\\) decreases with \\(c\\); condition (5) fails precisely\nfor the columns  \n\n\\[\nc=2,3,\\dots ,c_0,\\qquad\nc_0:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nHence at least  \n\n\\[\nt_{\\min}=c_0-2=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\dfrac{n}{2}-1, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n\\dfrac{n-1}{2}, & n\\text{ odd},\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nadditional vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines are **unavoidable**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Constructions attaining the bound  \n\nWe now exhibit configurations that use exactly the number of extra\nvertical lines given by (7) and consequently contain the maximal\npossible number of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.1  Even \\(n=2m\\;(m\\ge2)\\)\n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nTake the three lines (1) and the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,m).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(t=m-1=\\dfrac{n}{2}-1\\) such lines, i.e. exactly the number\nrequired by (7).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor each integer \\(d\\) with \\(0\\le d\\le m-3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_d:\\;y=x+d .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nAll lines \\(S_d\\) have slope \\(1\\); therefore they are sunny.\nThe number of sunny lines is \\(m-2=n-3\\).\n\n*Verification that every point of \\(I_n\\) is covered.*\n\n*   If \\(c\\le m\\) the vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains all interior points\n    of column \\(c\\).\n\n*   Let \\(c>m\\); write \\(c=m+r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le m-2\\).  \n    The interior points of this column are \\((c,b)\\) with\n    \\(2\\le b\\le m-r\\).  \n    Their differences \\(b-c\\) run through the set\n    \\(\\{-r-1,-r,\\dots ,-2\\}\\).  \n    For a point \\((c,b)\\) put \\(d=-(b-c)\\); then \\(0\\le d\\le m-3\\) and\n    the point satisfies \\(b=x+d\\), i.e. it lies on the sunny line\n    \\(S_d\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(I_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines\n(8)\u2013(9).  The configuration consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly\n\\(n-3\\) of which are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.2  Odd \\(n=2m+1\\;(m\\ge2)\\)\n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nAgain we keep the three lines (1) and now take the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,m+1).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(t=m=\\dfrac{n-1}{2}\\) extra vertical lines, which equals the\nlower bound (7) for odd \\(n\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_d:\\;y=x+d\\qquad(d=0,1,\\dots ,m-3).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are sunny; their number is \\(m-2=n-4\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nThe argument is identical to the even case.  \nFor a column \\(c\\le m+1\\) the vertical line \\(x=c\\) covers the whole\ncolumn.  \nFor a column \\(c>m+1\\) write \\(c=m+1+r\\;(1\\le r\\le m-2)\\); the same\ncomputation shows that each interior point of this column lies on a\nsunny line \\(S_d\\) with \\(d\\in\\{0,\\dots ,m-3\\}\\).\n\nHence we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(n-4\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.3  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(P_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nThe three distinct sunny lines  \n\n\\[\ny=x,\\qquad y=2x-1,\\qquad y=3x-5\n\\]\n\ncover the three points, so every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) is realizable\n(by turning some of the lines into vertical or horizontal ones).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Obtaining all smaller values of \\(k\\)\n\nStarting from the constructions of \u00a7\u00a7\u202f4.1\u20134.3 (which give the maximal\npossible number of sunny lines), we may replace any sunny line by an\nadditional non\u2011sunny line (for instance a vertical line that does not yet\nappear).  Such a replacement never destroys the covering property,\nbecause the new non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one side of the triangle\nand therefore already contains many points of \\(P_n\\).  By performing\n\\(k_{\\max}-k\\) such replacements we obtain a configuration with exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.  Consequently **every** integer between \\(0\\) and the\nmaximal value listed in \u00a7\u202f4 is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Optimality of the bounds  \n\nAssume that a configuration with more sunny lines than those given in\n\u00a7\u202f4 existed.  Then fewer than the numbers of extra vertical lines prescribed\nby (7) would be present.  Consequently there would be a column \\(c\\)\nwith height \\(h_c>n-3\\) and without a vertical line.  As argued in\n\u00a7\u202f3, a sunny line can meet that column in at most one point, so at least\n\\(h_c\\) distinct sunny lines would be required\u2014contradicting the fact that\nonly \\(n-3\\) sunny lines are available.  Hence the bounds are sharp.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Final answer  \n\nCollecting the results of \u00a7\u00a7\u202f4\u20136 we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{ll}\nn=3:&\\displaystyle\\{0,1,2,3\\};\\\\[2mm]\nn\\ge4\\text{ even}:&\\displaystyle\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-3\\};\\\\[2mm]\nn\\ge5\\text{ odd}:&\\displaystyle\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-4\\}.\n\\end{array}}\n\\]\n\nThese are precisely the non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist\n\\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem."

