points\n     \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  Using the three lines\n     \\[\n     L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n     L_2:\\;y=2x-1,\\qquad\n     L_3:\\;y=3x-5\n     \\]\n     (all sunny) we cover the three points, so every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\)\n     is attainable (by replacing any of the three lines with one of the\n     non‑sunny lines in (1)).\n\n6. **All smaller values of \\(k\\) are attainable.**  \n   Starting from the constructions above (which give the maximal possible\n   number of sunny lines), replace any sunny line by an additional\n   non‑sunny line (for instance a vertical line that does not yet appear).\n   Such a replacement never destroys the covering property because the\n   new line is parallel to a side of the triangle and therefore already\n   contains many points of \\(P_n\\).  By performing\n   \\(k_{\\max}-k\\) replacements we obtain a configuration with exactly\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Consequently every integer between \\(0\\) and the\n   maximal value listed in step 5 is realizable.\n\n7. **Optimality of the bounds.**  \n\n   *Three non‑sunny lines are unavoidable.*  \n   If a side of the triangle (say \\(X\\)) were covered only by sunny\n   lines, each such line could meet \\(X\\) in at most one point, so at\n   least \\(|X|=n\\) lines would be needed, contradicting the total of\n   \\(n\\) lines.  Hence a line parallel to each side is necessary; this\n   yields the general upper bound \\(k\\le n-3\\).\n\n   *For odd \\(n\\) a fourth non‑sunny line is necessary.*  \n   Assume that only the three lines (1) are used and that the remaining\n   \\(n-3\\) lines are sunny.  Consider the column \\(x=2\\).  It contains\n   \\(n-3\\) interior points (all points \\((2,b)\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-1\\)).\n   Because no vertical line is present in this column, each of the\n   \\(n-3\\) sunny lines can contribute at most one of those points.\n   Hence **all** \\(n-3\\) sunny lines must pass through distinct points of\n   this column.  Consequently none of them can pass through any other\n   interior point of the column \\(x=3\\), whose interior points are\n   \\((3,2),\\dots ,(3,n-2)\\) – there are \\(n-4\\) of them, but only\n   \\(n-4\\) sunny lines remain after the one already used for \\((2,n-1)\\).\n   A careful counting shows that when \\(n\\) is odd the column\n   \\(x=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) contains \\(\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) interior points,\n   while the number of sunny lines available after covering the first\n   column is only \\(\\frac{n-3}{2}\\); thus at least one interior point of\n   this middle column would stay uncovered – a contradiction.\n   Therefore a fourth non‑sunny line is required, giving the sharper\n   bound \\(k\\le n-4\\) for odd \\(n\\).\n\n   The constructions of step 5 meet these bounds, so they are optimal.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nP_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\)\nand \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Three unavoidable non‑sunny lines\n\nDefine the three families of points that lie on the sides of the\nright‑triangle determined by \\(P_n\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nX&=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\\\\nY&=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\\\\nZ&=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1,\\ a,b\\ge1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nIf a line is sunny, it is not parallel to any of the three sides,\nhence it meets each of the sets \\(X,Y,Z\\) in **at most one point**.\nConsequently, to cover all points of \\(X\\cup Y\\cup Z\\) we need at least\none line parallel to each side.  We fix the three non‑sunny lines\n\n\\[\nL_V:x=1,\\qquad L_H:y=1,\\qquad L_D:x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThese three lines already cover every point of \\(X\\cup Y\\cup Z\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Interior points\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nI_n=P_n\\setminus (X\\cup Y\\cup Z)\n     =\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge2,\\ a+b\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|I_n|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe remaining \\(n-3\\) lines must cover all points of \\(I_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Upper bounds for the number of sunny lines\n\nBecause the three lines in (1) are non‑sunny, at most \\(n-3\\) of the\n\\(n\\) lines can be sunny:\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-3 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(n\\) is odd we can improve this bound.\n\nAssume \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd) and that only the three lines (1) are used.\nConsider the column \\(x=2\\).  It contains the interior points  \n\n\\[\n(2,2),(2,3),\\dots ,(2,2m-1) ,\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(2m-1=n-3\\) points.  Because there is no vertical line in this\ncolumn, each of the \\(n-3=2m-1\\) sunny lines can contribute at most one\nof those points, so **all** sunny lines must pass through distinct points\nof this column.\n\nNow look at the middle column \\(x=m+2=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\).  Its interior\npoints are  \n\n\\[\n(m+2,2),\\;(m+2,3),\\dots ,\\;(m+2,m+1),\n\\]\n\nexactly \\(m\\) points.  After the previous step one sunny line has already\nbeen used for the point \\((2,2m-1)\\); the remaining \\(2m-2\\) sunny\nlines can cover at most \\(2m-2\\) points of the middle column, but we\nneed \\(m\\) points with \\(m>2m-2\\) when \\(m\\ge2\\) (i.e. \\(n\\ge5\\)).\nThus a contradiction arises, showing that with odd \\(n\\) at least **four**\nnon‑sunny lines are necessary.  Hence\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-4\\qquad\\text{for odd }n\\ge5 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Constructions attaining the bounds\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  Even \\(n=2m\\;(m\\ge2)\\)\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nTake the three lines (1) and the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_c:\\;x=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,m).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(m-1=\\dfrac{n}{2}-1\\) such lines.\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor each integer \\(d\\) with \\(0\\le d\\le m-3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_d:\\;y=x+d .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(S_d\\) have slope \\(1\\); therefore they are sunny.  Their number is\n\\(m-2=n-3\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \n\n*Columns \\(c\\le m\\).*  The vertical line \\(V_c\\) contains every interior\npoint of column \\(c\\).\n\n*Columns \\(c>m\\).*  Write \\(c=m+r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le m-2\\).  \nAn interior point of this column has the form \\((c,b)\\) where  \n\n\\[\n2\\le b\\le n+1-c=m-r+1 .\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nd:=m-1-b\\qquad\\bigl(0\\le d\\le m-3\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\nb = c+d-(m-1),\n\\]\n\nso \\((c,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(y=x+d\\); consequently it lies on the\nsunny line \\(S_d\\).  Hence every interior point of every column is\ncovered.  Together with the three lines (1) all points of \\(P_n\\) are\ncovered, and exactly \\(n-3\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  Odd \\(n=2m+1\\;(m\\ge2)\\)\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nTake (1) and the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_c:\\;x=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,m+1).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(m=\\dfrac{n-1}{2}\\) such lines.\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor each integer \\(d\\) with \\(0\\le d\\le m-3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_d:\\;y=x+d .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nAll are sunny; their number is \\(m-2=n-4\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nThe argument is identical to the even case.  For a column\n\\(c>m+1\\) write \\(c=m+1+r\\;(1\\le r\\le m-2)\\); an interior point is\n\\((c,b)\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n+1-c=m-r+2\\).  Putting\n\\(d:=m-1-b\\) (which lies in \\([0,m-3]\\)) gives\n\\(b=c+d-(m-1)\\), i.e. the point belongs to the sunny line \\(S_d\\).\nThus all interior points are covered, and the configuration contains\nexactly \\(n-4\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(P_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nThe three distinct sunny lines  \n\n\\[\ny=x,\\qquad y=2x-1,\\qquad y=3x-5\n\\]\n\ncover the three points, so every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) is realizable\n(by replacing any of the three lines with one of the non‑sunny lines in\n(1)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Obtaining every smaller value of \\(k\\)\n\nIn the constructions of §§ 4.1–4.3 the number of sunny lines is maximal.\nIf we replace any sunny line by an additional non‑sunny line (for\ninstance a vertical line that does not yet appear), the covering property\nremains valid because a non‑sunny line is parallel to a side of the\ntriangle and therefore already contains many points of \\(P_n\\).  By\nperforming \\(k_{\\max}-k\\) such replacements we obtain a configuration with\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Hence every integer between \\(0\\) and the\nmaximal value listed in step 4 is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nCombining the upper bounds (4)–(5) with the explicit constructions of\n§ 4 and the reduction argument of § 5 yields the complete description of\nthe admissible numbers of sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{ll}\nn=3:&\\{0,1,2,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\nn\\ge4\\text{ even}:&\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-3\\},\\\\[2mm]\nn\\ge5\\text{ odd}:&\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-4\\}.\n\\end{array}}\n\\]\n\nThus the answer is proved."
