ur** non\u2011sunny lines are necessary.  Hence  \n> \\[\n> k\\le n-4\\qquad\\text{for odd }n\\ge5 . \\tag{5}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Critical Error 3:* The \u201cmiddle column\u2019\u2019 is misidentified.  For \\(n=2m+1\\) we have \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}=m+1\\), not \\(m+2\\).\n\n*Critical Error 4:* The claimed number of interior points in that column is wrong.  The true middle column \\(x=m+1\\) contains \\(m-1\\) interior points (those with \\(b=2,\\dots,m\\)), not \\(m\\).\n\n*Critical Error 5:* The inequality \u201c\\(m>2m-2\\) when \\(m\\ge2\\)\u201d is false; for \\(m\\ge2\\) we have \\(m\\le2m-2\\).\n\n*Justification Gap:* Because the preceding statements are incorrect, the derived contradiction does not follow.  The bound \\(k\\le n-4\\) for odd \\(n\\) is therefore not proved.\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Constructions attaining the bounds  \n\n##### 5.1  Even \\(n=2m\\;(m\\ge2)\\)\n\n> \u201cTake the three lines (1) and the vertical lines  \n> \\[\n> V_c:\\;x=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,m).\n> \\tag{6}\n> \\]  \n> There are \\(m-1=\\dfrac{n}{2}-1\\) such lines.  \n> For each integer \\(d\\) with \\(0\\le d\\le m-3\\) define  \n> \\[\n> S_d:\\;y=x+d .\n> \\tag{7}\n> \\]  \n> All \\(S_d\\) have slope \\(1\\); therefore they are sunny.  Their number is \\(m-2=n-3\\).\u201d\n\n*Correct counting of lines.*  The total number of lines is indeed \\(n\\).\n\n> \u201cCoverage.  \n> *Columns \\(c\\le m\\).*  The vertical line \\(V_c\\) contains every interior point of column \\(c\\).  \n> *Columns \\(c>m\\).*  Write \\(c=m+r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le m-2\\).  \n> An interior point of this column has the form \\((c,b)\\) where  \n> \\[\n> 2\\le b\\le n+1-c=m-r+1 .\n> \\]  \n> Set  \n> \\[\n> d:=m-1-b\\qquad\\bigl(0\\le d\\le m-3\\bigr).\n> \\]  \n> Then  \n> \\[\n> b = c+d-(m-1),\n> \\]  \n> so \\((c,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(y=x+d\\); consequently it lies on the sunny line \\(S_d\\).\u201d\n\n*Critical Error 6 (Algebraic mistake):* From the definition \\(d=m-1-b\\) we have \\(b=m-1-d\\).  Substituting \\(c=m+r\\) gives  \n\\[\nc+d-(m-1)= (m+r)+(m-1-b)-(m-1)= m+r-b .\n\\]  \nThus the claimed equality \\(b=c+d-(m-1)\\) would require \\(b=m+r-b\\), i.e. \\(2b=m+r\\), which is **not** satisfied in general.  Consequently the point \\((c,b)\\) does **not** lie on the line \\(y=x+d\\) in general.\n\n*Concrete counter\u2011example:* For \\(n=6\\) (\\(m=3\\)) the construction yields the sunny line \\(y=x\\) and vertical lines \\(x=2,3\\).  The interior point \\((4,2)\\) (column \\(c=4>m\\)) is not on any line \\(y=x+d\\) with \\(d\\in\\{0\\}\\) and is not on any vertical line, so it remains uncovered.  Hence the construction fails to satisfy the covering requirement.\n\n*Conclusion:* The even\u2011\\(n\\) construction does **not** work; the proof of coverage is invalid.\n\n##### 5.2  Odd \\(n=2m+1\\;(m\\ge2)\\)\n\n> \u201cTake (1) and the vertical lines  \n> \\[\n> V_c:\\;x=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,m+1).\n> \\tag{8}\n> \\]  \n> There are \\(m=\\dfrac{n-1}{2}\\) such lines.  \n> For each integer \\(d\\) with \\(0\\le d\\le m-3\\) define  \n> \\[\n> S_d:\\;y=x+d .\n> \\tag{9}\n> \\]  \n> All are sunny; their number is \\(m-2=n-4\\).\u201d\n\n*Correct counting of lines* (total \\(3+m+(m-2)=2m+1=n\\)).\n\n> \u201cCoverage. The argument is identical to the even case.  For a column \\(c>m+1\\) write \\(c=m+1+r\\;(1\\le r\\le m-2)\\); an interior point is \\((c,b)\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n+1-c=m-r+2\\).  Putting \\(d:=m-1-b\\) (which lies in \\([0,m-3]\\)) gives \\(b=c+d-(m-1)\\), i.e. the point belongs to the sunny line \\(S_d\\).\u201d\n\n*Critical Error 7 (Same algebraic mistake as in the even case):* The derivation of the equality \\(b=c+d-(m-1)\\) is incorrect; the same computation shows that it would require \\(2b = 2m+1+r\\), which is not generally true.  \n\n*Concrete counter\u2011example:* For \\(n=7\\) (\\(m=3\\)) the construction provides vertical lines \\(x=2,3,4\\) and a single sunny line \\(y=x\\).  The interior point \\((5,2)\\) (column \\(c=5>m+1\\)) is not on any of the given lines, so the covering fails.\n\n*Conclusion:* The odd\u2011\\(n\\) construction is also invalid.\n\n##### 5.3  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\n> \u201c\\(P_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n> The three distinct sunny lines  \n> \\[\n> y=x,\\qquad y=2x-1,\\qquad y=3x-5\n> \\]  \n> cover the three points, so every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) is realizable (by replacing any of the three lines with one of the non\u2011sunny lines in (1)).\u201d\n\n*Critical Error 8:* The three listed lines do **not** cover the point \\((1,2)\\).  Substituting \\((1,2)\\) into each equation gives \\(2\\neq1\\), \\(2\\neq1\\), and \\(2\\neq-2\\).  Hence the claim that they cover all three points is false, and the conclusion for \\(n=3\\) is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Obtaining every smaller value of \\(k\\)\n\n> \u201cIn the constructions of \u00a7\u00a7\u202f4.1\u20134.3 the number of sunny lines is maximal.  If we replace any sunny line by an additional non\u2011sunny line (for instance a vertical line that does not yet appear), the covering property remains valid because a non\u2011sunny line is parallel to a side of the triangle and therefore already contains many points of \\(P_n\\).  By performing \\(k_{\\max}-k\\) such replacements we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\u201d\n\n*Justification Gap:* This reduction argument presumes the constructions of \u00a7\u00a7\u202f4.1\u20134.3 are correct.  Since those constructions fail to cover all points, the claim that \u201cthe covering property remains valid\u201d is unfounded.  No rigorous justification is provided that a replacement of a sunny line by a non\u2011sunny line preserves coverage.\n\n---\n\n#### 7.  Conclusion  \n\n> \u201cCombining the upper bounds (4)\u2013(5) with the explicit constructions of \u00a7\u202f4 and the reduction argument of \u00a7\u202f5 yields the complete description of the admissible numbers of sunny lines: \u2026\u201d\n\n*Invalid conclusion.*  Because the upper bound for odd \\(n\\) (5) is not proved and the constructions claimed in \u00a7\u202f4 are incorrect, the final classification of admissible \\(k\\) values is not established.\n\n---\n\n### Overall Assessment\n\nThe solution contains several **critical errors**:\n\n1. Incorrect counting of interior points and mis\u2011statement of the number of sunny lines for odd \\(n\\) (Section\u202f3).\n2. Wrong identification of the middle column and erroneous inequality leading to a false contradiction (Section\u202f3).\n3. Faulty algebra in the coverage arguments for both even and odd \\(n\\) constructions (Sections\u202f4.1 and\u202f4.2), resulting in uncovered points (explicit counter\u2011examples for \\(n=6\\) and \\(n=7\\)).\n4. Incorrect claim that three sunny lines cover all points for \\(n=3\\) (Section\u202f4.3).\n\nAdditionally, there are **justification gaps** where statements are asserted without sufficient proof (Sections\u202f1,\u202f3,\u202f5).  \n\nBecause the core constructions do not satisfy the problem\u2019s covering requirement and the claimed bound for odd \\(n\\) is not established, the solution does **not** solve the problem.  Hence the overall verdict is that the solution is invalid."

